message.content:
Design an implementation plan for upgrading three placeholder opcodes in mOMonadOS's kernel tick loop to real SIXTEEN_3-trilattice semantics, replacing their current B4 stand-in behavior. Do not write any code — this is planning only, output a detailed implementation plan as your response.

## Background (already verified directly, do not re-derive)

`mOMonadOS/src/kernel.rs`'s `Kernel::tick()` (lines 244-421) runs one IMASM token per tick through a `match tok { ... }` block (lines 271-393) that reads/writes three fields owned directly by `Kernel`: `memory: B4Memory` (4096 cells, 2 bits each, packed), `stack: B4Stack` (256-deep), `registers: B4Registers` (8 registers + an `engagr: bool` flag). All three types live in `src/belnap.rs` and store `B4` (a 4-valued enum: N/T/F/B).

Three token variants already exist in the `Token` enum and in this match block, but are literal stand-ins, explicitly flagged as such in their own code comments:

```rust
Token::Fsplit3 => {
    // 3-way fork (T/F/I arms). In B4 context, same as Fsplit.
    let v = self.stack.peek();
    let ffuse_ip = self.find_matching_ffuse3(self.ip);
    let resume = if ffuse_ip + 1 >= self.program.len() { 0 } else { ffuse_ip + 1 };
    self.push_fork(resume);
    if let Some(frame) = self.fork_top_mut() { frame.right_val = v; frame.right_set = true; }
    self.stack.push(v);
}
Token::Ffuse3 => {
    // 3-way fuse. In B4 context, same as Ffuse.
    let left = self.stack.pop();
    if let Some(frame) = self.pop_fork() {
        let right = if frame.right_set { frame.right_val } else { B4::N };
        self.stack.push(b4_join(left, right));
        next_ip = frame.resume_ip;
    } else { self.stack.push(left); }
}
Token::Evali => {
    // Evaluate on Information axis: keep B (both), everything else -> N.
    let v = self.stack.pop();
    self.stack.push(if v == B4::B { B4::B } else { B4::N });
}
```

`ForkFrame` (kernel.rs, near the top) is:
```rust
struct ForkFrame {
    resume_ip: usize,
    right_val: B4,
    right_set: bool,
}
```

The real trilattice these three opcodes are named after — SIXTEEN_3, the Shramko-Wansing carrier `P({T,F,t,f})`, 16 states — already exists as a complete, tested implementation in the sibling crate `MoDoT/imasm_core/src/imasm16_3.rs`, which mOMonadOS already depends on (`Cargo.toml`: `imasm_core = { path = "../MoDoT/imasm_core", default-features = false }`) and already imports from in four other files (`counterfactual.rs`, `ctc_loom.rs`, `parasm.rs`, `vita.rs`) — but only ever as a flat, standalone word-verdict pipeline (`parse_glyph_word` → `tri_ancestral_verdict`/`run_word_register`), never wired into `Kernel`'s live tick loop or its B4-typed stack/registers/memory. `imasm_core::check`'s graph/closure-analysis surface (`Graph`, `frobenius_closures`, `match_pairs`, `from_sequence`) has never been touched by mOMonadOS at all — only its flat `check::word_verdict` convenience function has (via vita.rs, sk_forge.rs, prooflift.rs).

The real `Reg16_3` type (in `imasm_core::imasm16_3`) is a real subset of `{T, F, t, f}` (16 states via a bitmask), with real methods already implemented and tested there:
- `union(self, o: Reg16_3) -> Reg16_3` — the μ (fuse) operation
- `truth_part(self) -> Reg16_3`, `falsity_part(self) -> Reg16_3` — pass-gates
- `constructive_part(self) -> Reg16_3` — projects onto the constructive {T,F} sub-carrier
- `info_part(self) -> Reg16_3` — projects onto the information/{t,f} sub-carrier
- `invol(self) -> Reg16_3` — the AREV involution (two disjoint bit-swaps: T↔F on the constructive layer, t↔f on the information layer)

The reference implementation for how a REAL 3-arm fork should split a value (already verified working, in `MoDoT/ask_native/src/imasm.rs`'s `gate_out` function, used by that project's `eval16`/`flow16` commands, never ported to mOMonadOS) is:
```rust
fn gate_out(tok: Token, x: Val, seed: Val, slot: usize, fan: usize) -> Val {
    match tok {
        Token::Vinit => seed,
        t if t.is_brancher() => {
            if fan >= 3 {
                match slot {
                    0 => x.constructive_part().truth_part(),
                    1 => x.constructive_part().falsity_part(),
                    _ => x.info_part(),
                }
            } else if slot == 0 { x.truth_part() } else { x.falsity_part() }
        }
        Token::Evalt => x.truth_part(),
        Token::Evalf => x.falsity_part(),
        Token::Arev => x.invol(),
        _ => x,
    }
}
```
So a real 3-way fork (fan=3, matching FSPLIT3's two arms plus the implicit "info" reading EVALI is meant to expose) produces three readings of one value: `constructive_part().truth_part()` (the T arm), `constructive_part().falsity_part()` (the F arm), and `info_part()` (the I/info arm) — not three independent copies of the same B4 value the way the current stand-in code does.

## What needs to be designed

1. How to promote a `B4` value (from the existing B4 stack) into a real `Reg16_3` value at `Fsplit3`, give its T-arm and F-arm genuinely different projections (`constructive_part().truth_part()` vs `constructive_part().falsity_part()`), carry the real `Reg16_3` on the fork frame (not just a `B4`), and at `Ffuse3` combine the two arms with real `union` (not `b4_join`) before collapsing the result back down to a `B4` for compatibility with the rest of the stack/machine, which remains B4-typed everywhere else. Need an explicit, justified embedding B4 -> Reg16_3 (e.g. N->{}, T->{T}, F->{F}, B->{T,F}) and a justified collapse Reg16_3 -> B4 back (e.g. by truth-and-falsity membership, matching however `imasm16_3`'s own FOUR-slice rendering already defines this, if it does — check `imasm_core/src/imasm16_3.rs` for an existing FOUR-collapse or "four_name"-equivalent function before inventing a new one).
2. How `Evali` should read the real `info_part()` of the current top-of-stack value (promoted to Reg16_3) rather than the current "keep B else N" approximation, and what B4 value the info_part() collapses back down to for the stack.
3. Whether `ForkFrame.right_val: B4` needs to become `Reg16_3` (widening the field for all forks, including ordinary 2-arm `Fsplit`/`Ffuse`, which would need a no-op promote/collapse to stay behaviorally identical) or whether a separate field/mechanism is cleaner so `Fsplit`/`Ffuse`'s existing behavior is provably untouched. Prefer whichever keeps `Fsplit`/`Ffuse` byte-for-byte unchanged with the least new surface area — this is a targeted fix to three opcodes, not a rewrite of the fork mechanism.
4. A minimal wrapper module (e.g. `src/sixteen_3.rs`) re-exporting exactly the `imasm_core::imasm16_3` items needed (following the exact existing pattern in `src/vox.rs`: `pub use vox_core::vox::*;` and `src/imasm_exec.rs`), so kernel.rs depends on a mOMonadOS-local module rather than reaching into `imasm_core` directly inline (check whether kernel.rs reaching into `imasm_core::imasm16_3` directly, matching the existing style already used by counterfactual.rs/ctc_loom.rs/vita.rs — i.e. `use imasm_core::imasm16_3::{...}` directly with no wrapper — would in fact be more consistent with the codebase's existing convention than adding a new wrapper module; decide based on precedent, not by default).
5. A concrete way to make this observable/testable from outside the kernel internals: a REPL surface (new command or extension of an existing Status-menu command like `registers`/`snapshot`) that shows the real Reg16_3 value the most recent Fsplit3/Ffuse3/Evali operated on — mirroring the existing pattern in `repl.rs` for `memory`/`registers`/`stack`/`heatmap` (parse optional numeric args, call a `k.`-prefixed accessor, format with a `.name()`-style method, print via `sprintln!`).
6. A concrete verification plan: a Rust unit test (or REPL-driven integration test, matching existing test conventions in kernel.rs/counterfactual.rs) that builds a small program using FSPLIT3/EVALT/EVALF/FFUSE3/EVALI, runs it through a real `Kernel` via `tick()`/`run()`, and confirms the two fork arms now carry genuinely distinct values where the old stand-in code would have forced them identical — plus a cross-check against the standalone `imasm_core::imasm16_3::{parse_glyph_word, run_word_register, tri_ancestral_verdict}` pipeline already used by counterfactual.rs, run on the same glyph word, to confirm the kernel's live tick-loop semantics agree with the standalone word-level analysis of the identical word (same instrument, two different execution contexts, checked against each other).

## Constraints

- Do not touch `Fsplit`/`Ffuse`/`Evalt`/`Evalf`'s existing 2-arm behavior at all — this plan is scoped to `Fsplit3`, `Ffuse3`, `Evali` only, plus whatever `ForkFrame` change is strictly required to support them.
- No new large memory subsystem (no parallel 4096-cell `Reg16_3`-typed memory bank) unless you find concrete evidence in the codebase that one is actually needed for this specific fix — the scope here is upgrading three opcodes' value semantics, not building a second B4Memory-equivalent.
- Keep the existing `#[cfg(not(feature = "hosted"))]` no_std-compatible constraints in mind; nothing in kernel.rs currently touches `std`.
- Follow the project's existing test/verification conventions (the `cargo build --release --target x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu --features hosted` / `cargo test` pattern already used throughout the crate) rather than inventing a new one.

Produce a detailed, concrete implementation plan: exact files, exact function/struct changes, the exact embedding/collapse functions with their definitions, the exact new or modified match arms, the REPL surface change, and the verification steps. Name every file you'd touch and the actual code you'd write in each.

